However, there are some steps you can take:

1. Check your spam folder: If you receive any suspicious attachments in your spam folder, delete them immediately.

2. Use anti-virus software: Install reputable anti-virus software on all devices that handle incoming mail (such as laptops, smartphones, etc.) so they can scan incoming messages before sending them out.

3. Disable macros: Some viruses use macro-based attacks to infect files with malicious code. To prevent this, disable macros in Outlook by going to File > Options > Advanced > Click "Security" tab and uncheck "Use macros."

4. Update your operating system: Make sure your operating system is up-to-date to ensure that any known vulnerabilities have been patched.

5. Be cautious when opening links: Always check if a link leads to a legitimate website before clicking on it. Also, avoid downloading anything from unknown sources unless you're confident about its origin.

6. Keep your computer updated: Regularly update your operating system and installed applications to protect against new threats.

7. Monitor your inbox regularly: Set up alerts to notify you of unusual activity such as large file downloads or unexpected attachments. This will help you identify potential threats quickly.


